Type `quit` to exit this chatbot.
APR stands for Annual Percentage Rate. It's a measure used to compare the cost of borrowing or credit across different products and lenders. The APR includes not only the interest rate but also any additional fees or costs associated with the loan, expressed as an annualized percentage.

For example, if you're looking at two loans with the same interest rate, but one has higher fees than the other, the APR will help you understand which option is actually cheaper in the long run. The APR gives a more complete picture of what it will cost to borrow money or use credit over the life of the loan.

It's important to note that while APR can be a useful tool for comparing different loans or credit products, it doesn't take into account all factors related to borrowing costs, such as potential changes in interest rates or fees. Therefore, it should always be used alongside other financial information when making decisions about borrowing or using credit.



